WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:01.290
Okay…

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.689
This isn’t good.

00:00:02.689 --> 00:00:07.550
I know you have no idea who I am, and have
no reason to care, but my lovely wife Brenda

00:00:07.550 --> 00:00:11.240
and I got ourselves into kind of a sticky
situation here.

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:14.300
We just wanted to go shopping, gosh darn it,
and now we’re trapped in here!

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:15.340
I think there are monsters…

00:00:15.340 --> 00:00:16.340
Oh god.

00:00:16.340 --> 00:00:20.050
This is horrible, I’m so scared - Not just
for my life, but for hers.

00:00:20.050 --> 00:00:21.050
And worst of all…

00:00:21.050 --> 00:00:23.280
Why does it feel so oddly familiar?

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:24.660
Okay, okay.

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:25.660
Gotta keep a cool head.

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:26.820
How did all this begin?

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:29.060
It’d been about a year since the Incident.

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:31.540
We don’t like to talk about it much.

00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:35.270
My psychiatrist tells me that, after I was
kidnapped by that mysterious international

00:00:35.270 --> 00:00:40.110
criminal organization and kept in their clutches
for 100 days, it’s likely I repressed most

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:42.531
of the memories to avoid the trauma.

00:00:42.531 --> 00:00:45.280
He told me it’s an extremely common reaction.

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:48.990
So common, in fact, that even Brenda can’t
fully remember the exact circumstances of

00:00:48.990 --> 00:00:53.260
my kidnapping - Just her immense gratitude
when the FBI brought me home.

00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:57.423
Eventually, we moved out of our old place
- too many dark spots - and into a new apartment,

00:00:57.423 --> 00:01:01.130
in a different town, in a different state.

00:01:01.130 --> 00:01:04.031
Things were good, until one day we realized:
We didn’t have a coffee table.

00:01:04.031 --> 00:01:07.300
Who can live without a coffee table these
days?

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:12.140
So we decided to make our way to the local
Ikea to buy a TRULSTORP coffee table.

00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:13.710
Brenda wouldn’t let me go alone.

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:17.970
She never lets me out of her sight anymore
since I was kidnapped.

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:21.490
Neither of us had been to Ikea in over a year,
so naturally, we found it easy to get lost

00:01:21.490 --> 00:01:22.490
in there.

00:01:22.490 --> 00:01:24.470
Those warehouses are confusing, after all.

00:01:24.470 --> 00:01:27.080
But this time, it was different.

00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:29.020
We were lost for hours on end.

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:32.540
We even started calling out for help, but
we never got any reply.

00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:36.730
And when, after what must have been closing
time, the overhead lights shut off.

00:01:36.730 --> 00:01:39.630
Well, we were less than calm about it…

00:01:39.630 --> 00:01:41.619
As a day passed, the two of us stuck together.

00:01:41.619 --> 00:01:45.510
We’d watched enough Scooby-Doo cartoons
in our youths to know that splitting up to

00:01:45.510 --> 00:01:49.220
cover more ground is the quickest way to get
irreparably lost.

00:01:49.220 --> 00:01:52.540
That being said, it’s not like sticking
together did us any better.

00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:57.509
We kept wandering through the different departments,
wondering if we’d passed that LOMMARP cabinet

00:01:57.509 --> 00:01:59.869
or that KOPPANG chest of drawers before.

00:01:59.869 --> 00:02:03.740
We walked until our legs ached, trying to
stay strong for each other.

00:02:03.740 --> 00:02:05.860
It’s impossible that we’d be trapped in
here.

00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:11.569
Sure, I know these warehouses can be a little
overcomplicated, but this is ridiculous!

00:02:11.569 --> 00:02:15.340
By the time the day ended - or at least, by
the time the store’s lights turned off - we

00:02:15.340 --> 00:02:19.099
were able to find a SLATTUM queen-sized bed
to rest for the night.

00:02:19.099 --> 00:02:21.359
Maybe we’ll be able to find a way out tomorrow…

00:02:21.359 --> 00:02:23.060
How long have we been in here?

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:25.349
I check the date on my phone: 72 hours.

00:02:25.349 --> 00:02:29.760
Though of course, there’s no internet or
cell service in here.

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:30.760
Naturally.

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:33.579
Brenda and I get up when the lights turn on
and start walking again.

00:02:33.579 --> 00:02:37.000
Surely anyone who walks for long enough has
to find something, right?

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:42.400
We both hold this hope in our hearts as we
pass what feels like miles of cheap, flat-pack

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:45.819
display furniture, wondering what on earth
is going on.

00:02:45.819 --> 00:02:48.310
Sometimes, we see figures moving in the distance.

00:02:48.310 --> 00:02:53.400
Part of me can’t help but wonder if they’re
really there, or if I’m just losing my mind

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:54.400
in here.

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:55.400
Difficult to say.

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:59.079
It feels hyperbolic to even express this thought,
but there’s something oddly frightening

00:02:59.079 --> 00:03:00.939
about the things I see out of the corner of
my eye.

00:03:00.939 --> 00:03:06.010
I start to get the sense that we’re not
alone in this strange place, and frighteningly,

00:03:06.010 --> 00:03:10.159
I don’t know if not being alone in here
is really such a good thing…

00:03:10.159 --> 00:03:11.849
At least we have each other in here.

00:03:11.849 --> 00:03:15.019
Oh god, oh god, oh god, things just got a
lot worse.

00:03:15.019 --> 00:03:19.040
So Brenda and I were walking down one of the
many latticed aisles of the kitchenware department

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:20.799
when the lights shut off.

00:03:20.799 --> 00:03:24.720
Thankfully, we’d picked up a pair of FRYELE
rechargeable flashlights, so moving in the

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:27.260
dark was no longer a dealbreaker for us.

00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:28.260
Goody goody.

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:32.150
Or at least, that’s what I thought until
we encountered something horrific in there.

00:03:32.150 --> 00:03:33.150
“Look!”

00:03:33.150 --> 00:03:34.150
Brenda said, pointing into the distance.

00:03:34.150 --> 00:03:35.169
“It’s a member of staff!”

00:03:35.169 --> 00:03:40.109
I couldn’t tell you why, but the second
Brenda said that, a chill went down my spine.

00:03:40.109 --> 00:03:45.049
It was like the lingering after-effects of
some memory I couldn’t recall.

00:03:45.049 --> 00:03:49.849
There was a silhouette in the distance, and
in the beam of the flashlight, I could make

00:03:49.849 --> 00:03:53.409
out the faint colors of an IKEA staff member’s
uniform.

00:03:53.409 --> 00:03:57.069
I knew right then that it was time to get
the hell out of here.

00:03:57.069 --> 00:04:00.890
But before I had a chance to say anything
to Brenda, the so-called “staff member”

00:04:00.890 --> 00:04:01.890
had already closed the distance.

00:04:01.890 --> 00:04:08.219
It ran into the beam of our flashlights, its
long, drooping arms and featureless face shining

00:04:08.219 --> 00:04:09.219
in the light.

00:04:09.219 --> 00:04:12.040
Instinctively, I threw myself into its path
to protect Brenda.

00:04:12.040 --> 00:04:14.840
It forced me to the ground and began beating
me with its fists.

00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:20.950
It kept repeating, in a kind of freakish,
robotic voice, “The store is now closed,

00:04:20.950 --> 00:04:22.690
please exit the building.”

00:04:22.690 --> 00:04:27.200
If Brenda hadn’t smashed it over the head
with a VATTENTÄT stainless steel kettle and

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:30.350
beat it to a pulp on the ground, I would have
been done for.

00:04:30.350 --> 00:04:33.280
So now we know: There are monsters in here.

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:34.280
Great.

00:04:34.280 --> 00:04:36.010
Food and water are becoming an issue.

00:04:36.010 --> 00:04:39.370
It’s not something we’ve needed to address
until now because of the healthy supply of

00:04:39.370 --> 00:04:43.380
snacks in Brenda’s purse, but there’s
a hard limit to things like that.

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:47.250
We realized we really needed to find food
in this place or we’d be doomed.

00:04:47.250 --> 00:04:50.810
But after the encounter with that monster,
we’re moving cautiously.

00:04:50.810 --> 00:04:53.450
Who knows how many of them are still out there?

00:04:53.450 --> 00:04:55.170
We can’t travel at night anymore.

00:04:55.170 --> 00:04:59.420
That’ll keep us safe, but it won’t do
anything about our hunger situation.

00:04:59.420 --> 00:05:01.900
It’s only a matter of time.

00:05:01.900 --> 00:05:02.900
Brenda’s a genius.

00:05:02.900 --> 00:05:06.130
I don’t know how I’d ever survive in this
terrible place without her, I’d probably

00:05:06.130 --> 00:05:07.930
die on day one on my own.

00:05:07.930 --> 00:05:11.860
She remembered that no Ikea is complete without
a little mini cafeteria where they serve a

00:05:11.860 --> 00:05:15.980
variety of Swedish dishes, including their
famous Ikea meatballs.

00:05:15.980 --> 00:05:20.820
All we’d need to do, in the otherwise sterile
environment of the Ikea, is follow the smell

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:23.640
until we found our way to sweet, meaty salvation.

00:05:23.640 --> 00:05:26.080
And that’s exactly what we did.

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:29.960
Eventually, this plan bore fruit and we found
our way to the cafeteria.

00:05:29.960 --> 00:05:33.700
I’d never been so happy to see crappy furniture
store food!

00:05:33.700 --> 00:05:37.520
We both grabbed a bowl full of meatballs when
suddenly, just before I could take a bite,

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:40.320
I felt an overwhelming wave of nausea.

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:44.080
Something about it felt so incredibly, unsettlingly
familiar.

00:05:44.080 --> 00:05:47.970
What the hell was going on here?

00:05:47.970 --> 00:05:50.740
Another day, another near-death experience.

00:05:50.740 --> 00:05:54.420
Our hearts leaped up into our throats when
we turned a corner and saw one of those faceless

00:05:54.420 --> 00:05:56.710
monsters just standing there in the next aisle.

00:05:56.710 --> 00:06:00.290
We were ready to either square up or run for
the hills.

00:06:00.290 --> 00:06:03.580
But oddly, this time, it didn’t even seem
to register our presence.

00:06:03.580 --> 00:06:08.830
It was almost like, during the day, they didn’t
have the same aggressive nature to them.

00:06:08.830 --> 00:06:11.000
Curioser and curioser.

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:15.240
Just like it said when it first attacked me,
I think these things really only do attack

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:16.890
when the store is closed.

00:06:16.890 --> 00:06:18.380
Guess that makes them “Lawful Evil.”

00:06:18.380 --> 00:06:20.150
God, I miss D&amp;D…

00:06:20.150 --> 00:06:24.300
We’re getting a little better at this whole
“freaky supernatural Ikea” thing.

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:28.610
Brenda figured, if there are monsters out
there, our best bet would be to arm up and

00:06:28.610 --> 00:06:30.090
protect ourselves.

00:06:30.090 --> 00:06:32.190
And hell, I’m not gonna disagree.

00:06:32.190 --> 00:06:36.820
We scavenged different parts of the store
to make ourselves a mini Ikea armory: VÄRDEFULL

00:06:36.820 --> 00:06:41.520
Meat Tenderizers, VÖRDA chef’s knives with
VÖRDA meat cleavers from the same set.

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:46.310
We also picked up some FRAKTA rope and tarpaulin,
and a pair of stylish VÄRLDENS travel backpacks

00:06:46.310 --> 00:06:51.120
to carry all our new Ikea swag in, in addition
to the food we’ve been keeping in the Ikea

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:52.310
PRUTA Tupperware packs.

00:06:52.310 --> 00:06:56.550
With the two of us together and all this equipment,
I feel like we’re ready for anything.

00:06:56.550 --> 00:07:02.060
In the following week, we discovered that
we were not, in fact, ready for anything.

00:07:02.060 --> 00:07:05.300
While what we would later call “The Eight
Days of Hell” started off rather pleasantly

00:07:05.300 --> 00:07:10.639
- the standard wandering, foraging, and sleeping
- it ended in a no holds barred fight for

00:07:10.639 --> 00:07:15.780
our lives when the lights turned off unexpectedly
while we were surrounded by maybe twenty members

00:07:15.780 --> 00:07:18.139
of the demonic Ikea “staff.”

00:07:18.139 --> 00:07:21.200
Even with our weaponry, we couldn’t fight
that many of them.

00:07:21.200 --> 00:07:23.430
Instead, we ran for our lives in the dark.

00:07:23.430 --> 00:07:28.410
That’s when Brenda tripped over a carelessly
discarded LILLABO three-piece toy train set.

00:07:28.410 --> 00:07:32.650
I ran back to help her up, but they were already
too close for us to make it out of there.

00:07:32.650 --> 00:07:35.470
Terrified, we hunkered down and waited to
die in each other’s arms.

00:07:35.470 --> 00:07:40.120
That’s when a series of expertly-placed
gunshots rang out through the air, and the

00:07:40.120 --> 00:07:44.490
staff members collapsed onto the ground, each
of their faceless heads perfectly perforated

00:07:44.490 --> 00:07:45.760
by a well-aimed bullet.

00:07:45.760 --> 00:07:50.450
We looked up to see the face of our savior:
A strange man holding a rifle and a bullet

00:07:50.450 --> 00:07:51.790
proof vest, grinning wildly.

00:07:51.790 --> 00:07:55.150
“Come with me if you want to live,” he
said.

00:07:55.150 --> 00:07:57.531
And we did, so we did.

00:07:57.531 --> 00:08:01.360
When we woke up the next morning after practically
passing out from the stress, Brenda and I

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:06.060
saw that we’d been shepherded into a miniature
makeshift campsite by our mysterious, well-armed

00:08:06.060 --> 00:08:07.060
good samaritan.

00:08:07.060 --> 00:08:11.271
He told us that his name was James, and he’d
been trapped in what he referred to as the

00:08:11.271 --> 00:08:13.250
“Infinite Ikea” for weeks now.

00:08:13.250 --> 00:08:16.920
Thankfully, he’d had his trusty rifle with
him, or he probably would have been killed

00:08:16.920 --> 00:08:18.919
by the Staff long before now.

00:08:18.919 --> 00:08:23.830
We decided, out of politeness, to not ask
him why he’d brought a rifle and a bulletproof

00:08:23.830 --> 00:08:26.050
vest into Ikea in the first place.

00:08:26.050 --> 00:08:27.780
We were just happy to be alive.

00:08:27.780 --> 00:08:31.650
We even gave him a few of our tubs of meatballs
as thanks for the daring assist.

00:08:31.650 --> 00:08:36.349
James told us there are others trapped in
here, some of which have even made full settlements.

00:08:36.349 --> 00:08:40.499
With his weaponry and our supplies, we might
even have a good chance of finding others

00:08:40.499 --> 00:08:42.990
out there, forming a group, and getting out.

00:08:42.990 --> 00:08:48.370
And that sounded like a great idea to Brenda
and I, so we were more than happy to tag along.

00:08:48.370 --> 00:08:52.360
Over the next 21 days, we explored more of
the “Infinite Ikea” with James.

00:08:52.360 --> 00:08:56.550
It certainly felt safer with him around, being
able to take on the Staff at a distance rather

00:08:56.550 --> 00:09:00.139
than risk being overpowered and killed in
a more traditional struggle.

00:09:00.139 --> 00:09:03.930
James is a nice guy - If a little strange.

00:09:03.930 --> 00:09:07.510
During our 21 day period of searching for
others, James killed a few different staff

00:09:07.510 --> 00:09:08.589
members during the nights.

00:09:08.589 --> 00:09:12.509
For Brenda and I, killing off attacking staff
members is a profound relief.

00:09:12.509 --> 00:09:13.509
But James?

00:09:13.509 --> 00:09:17.370
He seems to take an almost perverse pleasure
in killing them.

00:09:17.370 --> 00:09:20.230
But we do what we can to overlook that fact.

00:09:20.230 --> 00:09:24.899
At the end of the day, even if he’s a bit
of a freaky gun nut, he’s a valuable asset

00:09:24.899 --> 00:09:26.009
in a world like this.

00:09:26.009 --> 00:09:29.660
However, for days on end, our search for a
camp was fruitless.

00:09:29.660 --> 00:09:34.949
There were supposedly a huge number of communities
inside the Infinite Ikea, which often have

00:09:34.949 --> 00:09:36.779
their own unique cultures and hierarchies.

00:09:36.779 --> 00:09:40.139
It was hard to believe that all of this was
actually happening.

00:09:40.139 --> 00:09:43.620
Was James just making it all up and taking
us for a ride?

00:09:43.620 --> 00:09:47.290
But even if he was just crazy, what other
options did that leave us?

00:09:47.290 --> 00:09:48.529
We just had to keep going.

00:09:48.529 --> 00:09:51.620
But nothing would prepare us for the kind
of madness we were about to face…

00:09:51.620 --> 00:09:56.279
At some point during our journey, where we
were still searching for one of the supposed

00:09:56.279 --> 00:10:00.870
outposts hidden in the depths of the Ikea,
we were ambushed - but not by the Staff.

00:10:00.870 --> 00:10:07.339
No, it was a collection of human beings, wielding
knives and dressed in the Staff’s clothes,

00:10:07.339 --> 00:10:09.589
wearing their hollowed out heads like masks.

00:10:09.589 --> 00:10:14.480
There was maybe twenty of them when they got
the jump on us, surrounding us and neutralizing

00:10:14.480 --> 00:10:17.120
any advantage James’ gun could have given
us.

00:10:17.120 --> 00:10:21.029
These masked strangers identified themselves
as members of a nomadic gang calling themselves

00:10:21.029 --> 00:10:22.889
“The Sons of Vardagan.”

00:10:22.889 --> 00:10:27.120
They’d come from an outpost that had since
fallen, so at least we knew that James was

00:10:27.120 --> 00:10:30.339
right when he told us about communities out
there in the Ikea.

00:10:30.339 --> 00:10:33.309
And then something even stranger happened.

00:10:33.309 --> 00:10:37.000
One of the Sons of Vardagan pointed at me
and said he recognized me.

00:10:37.000 --> 00:10:40.670
I said that was impossible, and that I’d
never seen him before in my life.

00:10:40.670 --> 00:10:43.990
He brandished his knife and started getting
in my face, claiming I was lying.

00:10:43.990 --> 00:10:48.410
That’s when Brenda stepped forwards to defend
me, telling him to step off.

00:10:48.410 --> 00:10:52.220
As wonderful as her intention was, this just
made things worse.

00:10:52.220 --> 00:10:57.769
A few other members of the Sons of Vardagan
began ganging up on her, and that I simply

00:10:57.769 --> 00:10:58.769
couldn’t abide.

00:10:58.769 --> 00:11:04.119
Without even thinking, I threw an impulsive
punch, laying out one of the goons.

00:11:04.119 --> 00:11:08.249
That turned all heads my way, and gave James
an opportunity to take his chance.

00:11:08.249 --> 00:11:13.490
He turned his rifle towards some of them and
opened fire, killing several of them and scattering

00:11:13.490 --> 00:11:14.490
the rest.

00:11:14.490 --> 00:11:16.970
Maybe we were better at this whole survival
thing than we thought…

00:11:16.970 --> 00:11:20.910
Over the next couple of weeks, we continued
our search, our confidence bolstered by our

00:11:20.910 --> 00:11:22.629
win against the Sons of Vardagan.

00:11:22.629 --> 00:11:27.649
We made occasional stops, searching out cafeterias
to refuel our meatball supply.

00:11:27.649 --> 00:11:29.959
You need protein in the infinite Ikea.

00:11:29.959 --> 00:11:33.329
At some point during the journey, James asked
Brenda and me a strange question:

00:11:33.329 --> 00:11:36.050
“Have you ever heard of the SCP Foundation?”

00:11:36.050 --> 00:11:40.690
While neither of us could answer that question,
there was a strange, faint memory of that

00:11:40.690 --> 00:11:41.690
name.

00:11:41.690 --> 00:11:43.119
Was it some kind of charity?

00:11:43.119 --> 00:11:44.339
Maybe a government thing?

00:11:44.339 --> 00:11:49.350
I don’t know, but something about it, just
like so many other elements of this impossible

00:11:49.350 --> 00:11:52.079
situation, is eerily familiar to me.

00:11:52.079 --> 00:11:53.910
I just don’t understand.

00:11:53.910 --> 00:11:55.490
I just don’t get it.

00:11:55.490 --> 00:11:56.980
And it frightens me.

00:11:56.980 --> 00:11:59.630
68 days into our confinement, we found something
horrifying.

00:11:59.630 --> 00:12:04.679
One of the camps that James had alluded to
earlier, or at least, what was left of it.

00:12:04.679 --> 00:12:09.269
The walls were broken down and the courtyard
area of this makeshift community was littered

00:12:09.269 --> 00:12:10.459
with corpses.

00:12:10.459 --> 00:12:14.510
Brenda and I were horrified by the site of
what seemed like some kind of massacre.

00:12:14.510 --> 00:12:20.420
James just strode forward into the camp, observing
the aftermath of some unknown carnage.

00:12:20.420 --> 00:12:23.809
“Looks like we got here too late,” he
said.

00:12:23.809 --> 00:12:24.809
“Shame.

00:12:24.809 --> 00:12:25.809
Real shame.”

00:12:25.809 --> 00:12:30.550
James advised us to collect any resources
worth taking from the ransacked camp and moving

00:12:30.550 --> 00:12:31.550
on.

00:12:31.550 --> 00:12:33.399
No point crying over people we never even
knew.

00:12:33.399 --> 00:12:38.629
But looking at the bodies, part of me felt
as though I had known these people once, perhaps

00:12:38.629 --> 00:12:39.679
in another life.

00:12:39.679 --> 00:12:45.100
I got closer to one of these Deja vu-inducing
bodies, and noticed something strange: The

00:12:45.100 --> 00:12:47.029
body had gunshot wounds…

00:12:47.029 --> 00:12:49.519
Over the next four days, a nightmare erupted.

00:12:49.519 --> 00:12:53.350
After discovering the massacre at the camp,
I came to realize that James couldn’t be

00:12:53.350 --> 00:12:54.350
trusted.

00:12:54.350 --> 00:12:57.389
He was dangerous and unstable, and I had every
reason to believe he was the one behind all

00:12:57.389 --> 00:12:59.629
those killings.

00:12:59.629 --> 00:13:04.769
I relayed these facts to Brenda and told her
that we need to get away from him as quickly

00:13:04.769 --> 00:13:05.769
as possible.

00:13:05.769 --> 00:13:09.019
But when we attempted to escape, he caught
us and held us at gunpoint.

00:13:09.019 --> 00:13:11.519
He seemed intent on killing both of us.

00:13:11.519 --> 00:13:15.800
He was bigger, stronger, and better-armed
than the two of us, so all I could do was

00:13:15.800 --> 00:13:19.160
distract him and hope for an opportunity to
turn the tables.

00:13:19.160 --> 00:13:22.139
I asked him who he really was, and why he
was here.

00:13:22.139 --> 00:13:25.709
So, figuring he had nothing to lose, he told
us.

00:13:25.709 --> 00:13:30.920
Once upon a time, he had been a Mobile Task
Force operative, a kind of special ops guy,

00:13:30.920 --> 00:13:33.170
for that SCP Foundation group.

00:13:33.170 --> 00:13:37.740
He’d been one of the best, but after he’d
horrendously botched a mission - including

00:13:37.740 --> 00:13:42.990
intentionally murdering many civilians over
the course of his career, just for fun - he’d

00:13:42.990 --> 00:13:44.279
been demoted to D-Class.

00:13:44.279 --> 00:13:49.610
Which, to the best of my knowledge, seemed
to be human guinea pigs this SCP Foundation

00:13:49.610 --> 00:13:52.329
used for cruel experimentation.

00:13:52.329 --> 00:13:55.989
One of the experiments they used for him was
apparently this very location, the Infinite

00:13:55.989 --> 00:13:57.029
Ikea.

00:13:57.029 --> 00:14:02.790
He’d been in here for a long time now, with
guns and ammo he’d stolen from other Foundation

00:14:02.790 --> 00:14:05.319
operatives that had been trapped in here.

00:14:05.319 --> 00:14:07.309
In a sense, he loved it in here.

00:14:07.309 --> 00:14:13.470
He got to do his favorite thing of all - killing
people - with nothing to stop him.

00:14:13.470 --> 00:14:14.470
And now, he was going to do it to us.

00:14:14.470 --> 00:14:15.470
I hugged Brenda tight.

00:14:15.470 --> 00:14:18.449
I was sure that this would be our last moments.

00:14:18.449 --> 00:14:23.149
James leveled the barrel of his rifle at us
and prepared to fire, when two figures emerged

00:14:23.149 --> 00:14:25.769
from the darkness behind him, wielding knives
and hammers.

00:14:25.769 --> 00:14:27.949
It was a woman and a large man.

00:14:27.949 --> 00:14:32.340
They lunged at James and struck him, again
and again and again, until he collapsed to

00:14:32.340 --> 00:14:36.160
the ground, bleeding, dying, gone.

00:14:36.160 --> 00:14:40.439
These two people then looked up at me with
a sense of recognition and amazement.

00:14:40.439 --> 00:14:44.890
And at that moment, I realized that I recognized
them too: They were Vicky and Barry.

00:14:44.890 --> 00:14:45.890
I knew them.

00:14:45.890 --> 00:14:47.230
I’d been here before.

00:14:47.230 --> 00:14:49.989
Over the next several days, it all came back
to me.

00:14:49.989 --> 00:14:55.889
The hundred days I’d spent in the Infinite
Ikea before - well, going on 200 days now

00:14:55.889 --> 00:14:57.720
- The friends I’d made and lost.

00:14:57.720 --> 00:14:59.179
The strength I’d gained.

00:14:59.179 --> 00:15:03.600
I’d never been kidnapped - That had just
been a cover story formulated by the same

00:15:03.600 --> 00:15:07.079
SCP Foundation that’d produced that gun-toting
maniac, James.

00:15:07.079 --> 00:15:12.230
Brenda was amazed to hear all this, and I
was both amazed and delighted to see that

00:15:12.230 --> 00:15:13.679
Vicky and Barry had survived.

00:15:13.679 --> 00:15:17.399
We believed that Barry had died after leading
the Staff away from the encampment, but as

00:15:17.399 --> 00:15:20.269
it turns out, some people are just too badass
to kill.

00:15:20.269 --> 00:15:26.350
My memories were hitting me like a high-pressure
fire hose now, washing away the cobwebs and

00:15:26.350 --> 00:15:28.129
giving me a kind of clarity that I hadn’t
known in an entire year.

00:15:28.129 --> 00:15:32.750
And in that glorious moment, I remembered.

00:15:32.750 --> 00:15:34.470
I’d gotten out of here before.

00:15:34.470 --> 00:15:36.639
I could get us all out of here again.

00:15:36.639 --> 00:15:38.860
The route was somewhere inside me.

00:15:38.860 --> 00:15:41.059
I just needed to follow it.

00:15:41.059 --> 00:15:42.059
Day 200.

00:15:42.059 --> 00:15:45.879
I remembered somewhere, deep down, that I
needed to keep moving.

00:15:45.879 --> 00:15:47.629
I led Brenda, Vicky, and Barry behind me.

00:15:47.629 --> 00:15:50.749
They trusted me, and their trust would be
rewarded.

00:15:50.749 --> 00:15:54.779
Step after step, hour after hour, until eventually…

00:15:54.779 --> 00:15:55.899
We arrived.

00:15:55.899 --> 00:15:57.930
Light filtered through the glass double doors.

00:15:57.930 --> 00:15:59.709
A smile spread across my face.

00:15:59.709 --> 00:16:01.089
Our breath quickened.

00:16:01.089 --> 00:16:02.910
Barry began to tear up.

00:16:02.910 --> 00:16:06.449
After all this time, we’d finally found
our way out.

00:16:06.449 --> 00:16:10.149
We had officially survived 200 days in Ikea.

00:16:10.149 --> 00:16:15.110
As we stepped closer, the automatic doors
opened, as though it was congratulating us.

00:16:15.110 --> 00:16:18.069
We held hands and stepped out into the light
together.

00:16:18.069 --> 00:16:24.731
Now go check out “How To Actually Beat SCP-3008
The Infinite Ikea” and “Surviving Inside

00:16:24.731 --> 00:16:31.610
SCP-3008 The Infinite Ikea” for more adventures
from the scariest furniture store in the multiverse!

